WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.340
A good light microscope resolves a little over 200 nanometres.

2
00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:14.980
Two neurons touching could not be told apart from one continuous network, because

3
00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.520
the gap between them is far below that.

4
00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:21.500
Electrons have a much shorter wavelength than light.

5
00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:27.960
So electron microscopy can ask what the fine structure of a cell is: its membranes,

6
00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:32.720
junctions, vesicles and organelles, at nanometre scale.

7
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.520
First, fix the tissue.

8
00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:39.060
Glutaraldehyde mainly cross-links proteins.

9
00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:45.400
Osmium tetroxide reacts with membrane lipids, and it is heavy enough to add electron

10
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:51.620
density. The fixative must diffuse in, so slow infiltration can distort membranes.

11
00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:56.900
Then solvent, acetone or ethanol, replaces the water.

12
00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:01.900
Last, resin soaks in and sets into a hard plastic block.

13
00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:09.490
An ultramicrotome with a glass or diamond knife cuts sections roughly 60 to 80

14
00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:15.810
nanometres thick. Serial sections lie in ribbons on a grid, so one cell can be

15
00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:17.670
followed from section to section.

16
00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:23.870
Sections are usually stained with uranyl acetate then lead citrate, which add

17
00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:29.310
contrast. The beam passes through the stained section and is absorbed and scattered.

18
00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:34.910
The image is a flat projection of a thin slice of dead, prepared tissue.

19
00:01:35.783 --> 00:01:37.743
Freezing is another route.

20
00:01:38.503 --> 00:01:43.723
High-pressure freezing stops cell activity quickly and suppresses damaging ice

21
00:01:43.723 --> 00:01:49.543
crystals. Scanning electron microscopy images surfaces instead of slices.

22
00:01:50.763 --> 00:01:56.243
In freeze-fracture, the specimen is frozen and fractured, and a platinum-carbon

23
00:01:56.243 --> 00:02:01.903
replica is made. The replica, not the tissue, goes in the microscope, and shows

24
00:02:01.903 --> 00:02:05.223
planar views of the internal organisation of membranes.

25
00:02:06.167 --> 00:02:09.547
Here is a synapse, drawn as a schematic.

26
00:02:10.667 --> 00:02:12.167
Dark is where heavy metal bound.

27
00:02:13.187 --> 00:02:18.467
The two membranes stay separate across a cleft, so the synapse is a gap, not

28
00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:24.787
continuity. De Robertis and Bennett measured the cleft at 10 to 15 nanometres in

29
00:02:24.787 --> 00:02:29.767
1955. Palay found about 20 nanometres in 1956.

30
00:02:31.087 --> 00:02:36.867
Small vesicles crowd the presynaptic side, and the postsynaptic membrane is thickened.

31
00:02:37.117 --> 00:02:39.417
Fixation can create structures.

32
00:02:40.317 --> 00:02:45.637
In Bacillus cereus, conventional fixation gave pocket-like membrane invaginations

33
00:02:45.637 --> 00:02:48.877
that were absent after freezing and freeze substitution.

34
00:02:49.937 --> 00:02:54.137
The control is to confirm a structure with an independent preservation method.

35
00:02:55.077 --> 00:03:00.437
Every cell imaged is dead, and few cells are imaged in detail, so compare fixation

36
00:03:00.437 --> 00:03:02.437
methods and examine many cells.

37
00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:11.560
In 1954 Palade and Porter found the endoplasmic reticulum in situ in every cell type

38
00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:20.020
they examined. In 1955 Palade described dense particles, 100 to 150 angstroms,

39
00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:22.960
preferentially on its membranes in many cell types.

40
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:31.620
In 1973 Heuser and Reese stimulated frog neuromuscular junctions at 10 Hz and fixed

41
00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:39.700
samples. Membrane in vesicles fell 30% at 1 minute and 60% at 15 minutes, and a

42
00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:42.120
tracer entered cisternae, then vesicles.

43
00:03:43.083 --> 00:03:51.243
In 1972 Kerr, Wyllie and Currie described apoptosis as distinct from necrosis:

44
00:03:51.883 --> 00:03:56.483
condensation and breakup into membrane-bound, well-preserved fragments.

45
00:03:57.503 --> 00:04:03.503
Keep this rule. Conventional electron microscopy shows structure in dead, fixed

46
00:04:03.503 --> 00:04:05.303
tissue, never motion.

47
00:04:06.163 --> 00:04:09.203
Confirm a structure with an independent preservation method.
